WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:03.710
This is James Brouwer.

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.207
He lives in British Columbia, Canada.

00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:07.630
He’s a collector.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:09.293
Of records.

00:00:09.293 --> 00:00:11.762
Photographs.

00:00:11.762 --> 00:00:13.948
Vintage picture frames.

00:00:13.948 --> 00:00:17.232
JAMES BROUWER: All sorts of visual imagery
and ephemera....

00:00:17.232 --> 00:00:18.859
He also collects postcards.

00:00:18.859 --> 00:00:20.338
They’re in these boxes.

00:00:20.338 --> 00:00:23.313
BROUWER: And I'm sort of obsessive about this
in my collecting.

00:00:23.313 --> 00:00:30.000
BROUWER: I like that sort of visual arrangement of
sameness, repeating itself in different contexts.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.887
BROUWER: It could be anything from a figure 
standing in front of a mountain 

00:00:33.887 --> 00:00:36.529
and you see another one with a figure 
standing on a mountain 

00:00:36.529 --> 00:00:37.970
and a figure standing 
in front of a field.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.357
BROUWER: And pretty soon you start collecting 
onlooker postcards.

00:00:41.357 --> 00:00:42.630
The onlookers are cool.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.885
But this story isn’t about them.

00:00:44.885 --> 00:00:49.484
It’s about a type of image James didn’t
even realize he was collecting at first.

00:00:49.484 --> 00:00:53.370
BROUWER: I think when I got home and started
more and more going through my collection

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:58.452
and putting images together that I'm like...
you just see it.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.597
It’s the same sky.

00:01:02.597 --> 00:01:04.280
In Alberta, Canada.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.031
Redlands, California.

00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:07.868
And Juarez, Mexico.

00:01:07.868 --> 00:01:12.509
BROUWER:  I think what grabs in my mind is that
part of the cloud at the very far end looks

00:01:12.509 --> 00:01:14.609
like a little mouth of a creature.

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:17.179
It's something your brain grabs on to.

00:01:17.179 --> 00:01:22.140
Once you see it, the images suddenly 
take on a sort of, uncanny quality.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.084
So why would someone go through all the trouble
to make these places look the same?

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.793
This story started back in January.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:38.880
When Estelle, she produces Vox Earworm
and has made some of your favorite Vox videos

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.649
sent me a link on Slack.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:45.000
ESTELLE: Anyway, if you ever want to do a
darkroom on postcards… this one is cool,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.754
it’s the same stock sky on every postcard.

00:01:48.754 --> 00:01:50.453
It brought me to this Flickr page.

00:01:50.453 --> 00:01:54.290
And I sent a message asking about 
the same sky postcards.

00:01:55.573 --> 00:01:57.413
I met James over Zoom.

00:01:57.413 --> 00:02:01.338
BROUWER: I have them grouped with that 
little bit of cloud facing the other direction.

00:02:01.338 --> 00:02:05.619
BROUWER: Sometimes they shift it a little bit so 
that distinctive cloud formation is sometimes 

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:07.875
a bit off to the left or 
a bit off to the right.

00:02:07.875 --> 00:02:10.365
BROUWER: Sometimes it hugs 
the top of the frame

00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:14.292
sometimes it's more in 
the middle of the frame.

00:02:14.292 --> 00:02:18.890
There’s actually a couple of sky photos
that get repeated with slight position shifts

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.360
in James’s collection.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.209
And it’s pretty clear that these skies 
were not part of the original photos.

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:28.000
There’s the one James says looks 
like a sea creature.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.290
And one with a cloud that sort of reminds
me of the shape of Cuba.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:32.960
Sort of.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.734
Anyway, once I started looking 
for those skies

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:38.606
I came across them 
pretty easily on eBay

00:02:38.606 --> 00:02:41.878
and in antique stores 
in Brooklyn.

00:02:41.878 --> 00:02:46.014
I began building my own small collection 
of same-skies.

00:02:46.014 --> 00:02:49.074
But I wanted to go see James’s 
original collection.

00:02:49.074 --> 00:02:54.400
So it was at this point that I got on a plane
to beautiful British Columbia.

00:02:58.638 --> 00:03:00.058
Daaamn.

00:03:04.915 --> 00:03:09.111
BROUWER: So yeah, I just started scanning them,
cropping them and putting them up on Flickr.

00:03:09.111 --> 00:03:13.110
And now I think there's about eleven thousand
of them uploaded.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:18.050
BROUWER: So there's advertising, old age homes, 
windows, wax museums.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:21.424
There's some really strange wax museums that
are nicely preserved in postcards 

00:03:21.424 --> 00:03:24.130
and probably nowhere else.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:28.260
The bulk of James’s collection are what’s
known as “chrome-era postcards".

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:35.580
BROUWER: So a chrome card denotes a particular
period of postcard production, postwar, 

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:42.830
from the late 40s through to the present, essentially,
with glossy color photographs.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.420
And it takes its name from Kodachrome.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:51.400
Kodachrome was the first commercially viable
full color film, introduced by Kodak in 1935.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:55.950
These types of postcards, taken with the new
color film, circulated widely in North America

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.870
in the 1940s through the 1970s.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.660
With branding on the back like:

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.840
BROUWER: Plastichrome, Lusterchrome, 
things like that.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.730
Always with that word “chrome.”

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:12.110
To denote the shine and the gloss that you
see on these postcards and the punchy color.

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:17.009
By the time James started collecting in the
1980s, the glossy, mid-century chrome cards

00:04:17.009 --> 00:04:20.060
were just piled up in flea markets, 
largely overlooked.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.758
BROUWER: The postwar cards 
were considered more junky.

00:04:23.758 --> 00:04:25.510
It was something easy to collect 
for the cheap.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:26.514
And I’m like, sure I'll grab that.

00:04:26.514 --> 00:04:27.980
Looks cool, looks cool, looks cool. 

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:30.360
And pretty soon I'd amassed a lot of it.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.020
And I don't think I ever noticed at the time
that I was grabbing the cards that had 

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.900
the exact same sky repeated 
in different contexts.

00:04:37.833 --> 00:04:40.850
There’s a few things we know about 
the same-sky postcards.

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:43.869
Namely, that they all came from the same place.

00:04:43.869 --> 00:04:48.540
BROUWER: It appears that these same sky postcards
are all from one publishing company

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:49.870
Dexter Press.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.342
Dexter Press was one of the largest 
postcard manufacturers in the world 

00:04:53.342 --> 00:04:54.501
during the chrome era.

00:04:54.501 --> 00:04:58.790
And looking into their history provides a
clue about the same sky postcards.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:02.890
It was founded by this guy, Thomas A. Dexter,
in Pearl River, New York.

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:07.210
And, according to the back of this postcard
from National Post Card Week ‘86,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:12.150
Dexter was printing 4 million cards a day
at their peak, and pioneered the so-called

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.403
“Natural Color” printing process.

00:05:14.403 --> 00:05:19.574
“Natural Color” referred to a mechanical
process of printing postcards from color photographs.

00:05:19.574 --> 00:05:25.175
BROUWER: So it might be that this particular
publishing company had some reason to do it.

00:05:25.175 --> 00:05:30.440
It might be that it had a lot of visual punch
this white and the blue.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.326
Maybe they thought that’d
enlivened the image.

00:05:32.326 --> 00:05:35.710
Or it might have something to do with 
the company's other innovation.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:40.160
Dexter patented a process called “gang printing,”
which upped their printing capacity

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.680
and enabled them to take on print orders for
smaller postcard publishers.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:51.990
BROUWER: Maybe they thought it was some strange
way of marking Dexter Press's visual territory.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:55.000
Like a little signature of sorts
that no one would notice.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.440
And I’m going to be honest, that’s the
explanation I was hoping we’d end up with.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:00.780
That this was...

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:06.310
...a sort of little trademark that Dexter
would slide in to make it a Dexter image.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:08.420
Do you think that's a possibility?

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:09.480
BILL BURTON: No.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:12.042
After talking to James, I called Bill Burton.

00:06:12.042 --> 00:06:16.730
He’s the publisher of the online magazine
Postcard History, and I sent over two same-sky

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.290
postcards to ask him what he thinks.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.126
And his explanation made a lot of sense.

00:06:21.126 --> 00:06:24.736
BURTON: Dexter was the go-to guy 
to print chrome postcards.

00:06:24.736 --> 00:06:29.188
BURTON: He had very large presses, and 
he could print them at very high speeds.

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:30.493
BURTON: He had a big art department.

00:06:30.493 --> 00:06:34.330
He would offer to photo-correct 
any problems in the image.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:38.916
So if the sky for whatever reason didn’t
look the way a customer wanted it to…

00:06:38.916 --> 00:06:42.260
BURTON: The artist would cut out a mask that
would just go right up the telephone pole

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:45.783
and across it and down and along the roofline
or whatever it was.

00:06:45.783 --> 00:06:50.042
BURTON: Then you would put the sky behind it and 
you'd match the two of them up.

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:55.889
These two cards have the same image in the sky 
because they had stock images of skies.

00:06:55.889 --> 00:06:58.424
And most people wouldn't have known the difference.

00:06:58.424 --> 00:07:02.269
I mean, who goes to a store, buys three postcards
and then grabs a magnifying glass 

00:07:02.269 --> 00:07:03.869
and looks at what they got?

00:07:03.869 --> 00:07:04.869
Nobody.

00:07:04.869 --> 00:07:06.510
Well there is one person.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:09.631
BROUWER: And I still collect them and I still
love them and I still am 

00:07:09.631 --> 00:07:12.840
a champion of the chrome-era postcard.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.867
Dexter Press is long gone.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:18.914
And there’s really no way to know for sure
why they replaced some skies 

00:07:18.914 --> 00:07:22.070
and seemingly left others untouched.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:28.218
But they probably never anticipated that somebody,
someday, would put them all together.

00:07:28.218 --> 00:07:31.547
BROUWER: To me, it's not so much 
the grand mystery of why they did it

00:07:31.547 --> 00:07:35.876
because I think visually it creates 
something really remarkable.

00:07:35.876 --> 00:07:39.410
BROUWER: These cards that are meant to 
denote a particular place...

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:41.340
Like Grand Teton, Wyoming.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.674
Or McLeod’s Lake, British Columbia.

00:07:43.674 --> 00:07:48.154
BROUWER: ...when taken en masse, the differences
get washed away and so you get 

00:07:48.154 --> 00:07:52.320
this sort of typology or type that 
floats above the particulars beneath.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.970
And these images, which are often pretty bland
on their own, transform into something new.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:01.050
BROUWER: And it's very obvious with 
the same sky pictures,

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:06.419
You've got something arbitrary, like an amalgamation
of clouds, they start repeating and gaining

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:09.366
a strange significance just by 
virtue of being repeated.

00:08:09.366 --> 00:08:11.589
BROUWER: It makes the card not just what it is.

00:08:11.589 --> 00:08:14.148
BROUWER: It becomes something else.

00:08:15.178 --> 00:08:20.020
James mentioned a couple of figures from the
art world that inspired the look of his collection.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:22.030
Including pop artist Andy Warhol.

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:27.806
Whose wall of Campbell's soup cans and colorful
portraits of thee rich and famous, you might recognize.

00:08:27.806 --> 00:08:31.650
And two conceptual artists I didn’t know
about, but I’m glad I do now:

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.020
Bernd and Hilla Becher.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:37.860
Who photographed industrial architecture in
Western Europe starting in the 1950s.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.289
Their whole thing was about 
form and sequencing too.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:45.720
BROUWER: And the beautiful way they were arranged
so that the type of imagery would repeat 

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:51.131
with small differences in between, and just 
the visual impact of that is incredible.

00:08:51.131 --> 00:08:53.097
So I sort of wanted to reproduce that.

00:08:53.097 --> 00:08:56.640
There’s a link to James’s full collection
online in the description of this video.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.704
Plus a couple of links that I found helpful

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:00.987
if you want to do some postcard research 
of your own.

00:09:00.987 --> 00:09:01.740
Thanks for watching

